  "phylogeny": "Mesopotamia",
  "description": "48 of the 88 IAU constellations are consolidated by ancient Greeks from the originals of the ancient Babylonian, Egyptian and Assyrian. These were recorded in Ptolemy’s Almagest. Between 16th-17th c. AD, European astronomers added new discoveries made by the Europeans who explored the southern hemisphere. Eugène Delporte originally listed the 88 modern constellations on behalf of the IAU Commission 3 (Astronomical Notations), in 'Délimitation scientifique des constellations' (Delporte, 1930). In star maps it is common to mark line figures. However, the IAU defines a constellation by its boundary (in sky coordinates). Alan MacRobert's constellation patterns, drawn in green in the charts on the IAU website, were influenced by those of H. A. Rey (1952), but in many cases were adjusted to preserve earlier traditions. Constellations Mic and Men are not listed here: they don't have main stars nor lines marked. Exceptionally, the Latin constellation names also form the English gloss, since they are in common use in English, and a translation would be less precise.",
